msgid "The claim in words, for whoever reads the result."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:167
msgid ""
"An object may declare as many simulations as it likes, and two of them "
"can make opposite claims about one part in two worlds: ``float`` rises in"
" ``tank`` and falls in the default scene, which is a vacuum."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:171
msgid ""
"Relative names in a declaration -- ``sim-mujoco:mujoco``, ``:tilted`` -- "
"resolve from the package the object is in, not from where the command was"
" run, so ``pc test -P //...`` run at the root of a tree resolves each of "
"them the way ``pc sim`` in that package would."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:176
msgid ""
"``simulate:`` is not part of what the object is built from: declaring or "
"editing a simulation does not rebuild the object. The section's schema is"
" under :ref:`simulate`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:184
#, fuzzy
msgid "Scenes as worlds"
msgstr "8. 把场景作为世界"

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:186
msgid ""
"A simulation is never of an object alone: it is of an object *in a "
"world*. That world is an ordinary :ref:`scene <scenes>`, of any scene "
"type, with one thing about it that makes it a simulation scene -- it "
"takes the object as a parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:191
msgid "The default scene"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:193
msgid ""
"``//builtin/scene:subject`` holds the object being simulated and nothing "
"else. It states no gravity, so a run in it is under the engine's own -- "
"Earth's, along -Z -- and no medium, so it is a vacuum. The floor the "
"object stands on and the light it is drawn in are not part of the scene: "
"each engine's exporter writes its own (see :ref:`sim-engines`)."
msgstr ""

