"and its declaration, which gives each parameter a default so that the "
"scene on its own -- what ``pc render`` draws and ``pc ide view`` shows --"
" holds something:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:254
msgid ""
"A scene that declares no ``subject`` parameter cannot hold the object, "
"and a simulation that names one fails. Anything else the world needs -- a"
" fixture, a ramp, a bin -- is linked into the scene beside the subject, "
"like any other part of it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:260
msgid "Gravity, and the fluid a scene is filled with"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:262
msgid ""
"A scene may say what its world is like as well as where things are in it:"
" the gravity in it, and the fluid it is filled with."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:274
msgid ""
"``gravity:`` is a vector, so a world whose Z axis is not up says so, and "
"so does a ramp with no edge -- ``tilted`` above is a level floor under a "
"gravity that leans, which for sliding is the same thing. ``medium:`` "
"names a :ref:`material <materials>`, and its ``density`` and "
"``viscosity`` are what the engine drags and buoys a body with; "
"``//pub/std/manufacturing/material/fluid`` catalogues ``air``, ``water`` "
"and ``seawater``. Both are optional, and leaving them out is what every "
"engine assumes: its own gravity, in a vacuum. The keys, their units and "
"what is refused are in :ref:`scenes`; what each engine makes of a medium "
"is in :ref:`sim-engines`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:284
msgid ""
"A ``simulate:`` cannot ask the default scene for water. The fluid a part "
"is simulated in is a fact about the world, like the fixture it stands on,"
" so a package that wants its part under water writes a scene that is "
"under water, as ``tank`` is. A medium parameter on the built-in scene "
"would be a second way of saying the same thing, resolved against "
"``//builtin/scene`` rather than against the package that wrote it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:291
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"**Which gravity a run is under**, in order: the declaration's own "
"``params: {gravity: [...]}``, for that run; the scene's ``gravity:``; the"
" engine's default (MuJoCo's 9.81 m/s², SDFormat's 9.8, both along -Z)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:299
#, fuzzy
msgid "Materials and physical properties"
msgstr "接触与表面属性"

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:301
msgid ""
"Whether a stack stands, a float rises or a block slides is decided by "
"what the parts are made of as much as by their shape. The model an engine"
" is handed carries, for every part, its mass, its centre of mass and its "
"inertia, its sliding friction, and -- for a scene filled with a fluid -- "
"the volume it displaces and where that volume is centred. PartCAD works "
"each of them out once, the same way for every engine, for ``pc info`` and"
" for the URDF export, so what ``pc info`` says a part weighs is what a "
"simulation of it weighs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:310
msgid "What a part is made of"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:312
msgid ""
"A :ref:`material <materials>` is a set of facts about a substance, "
"declared in a ``materials:`` section and shared like any other object. A "
"simulation reads three of them: ``density``, in kg/m³, which gives a part"
" its mass, centre of mass and inertia; ``mu``, the dimensionless "
"coefficient of sliding friction; and ``viscosity``, in Pa·s, which only a"
" fluid states. The standard catalogues listed there cover the usual "
"plastics, metals and fluids."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:319
msgid ""
"A part names its material with the ``material`` parameter, on the part "
"types that make one homogeneous body (see :ref:`parameters`), and the "
"part type records the answer as the ``material`` property of the shape it"
" makes, which is what every exporter reads. That is also how two parts "
"built by one script can be made of different things, as ``block`` and "
"``block_ptfe`` are in ``feature_simulate``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:338
msgid "Stating what the material cannot say"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:340
msgid ""
"A part states a value under ``properties: physics:`` when its geometry "
"and its material cannot say it. A sealed float is drawn as the solid it "
"displaces, but it is hollow, so it states its mass; a float with a lead "
"weight in its bottom also states where it balances:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:356
msgid ""
"A stated value beats the material, because a measured part beats the "
"substance it is made of, and whatever the part does not state is still "
"worked out: the float above turns the way its solid says it does, at the "
"weight it states."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:361
msgid "Where each value comes from"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:363
msgid ""
"Each value is the first of these that applies -- what the part states; "
"what its solid comes to at the density of its material; and, for an "
"export only, the export's ``density`` parameter or else 2700 kg/m³ "
"(aluminium). Friction is what the part states, or else its material's "
"``mu``, or else the engine's default. The mass, the centre of mass and "
"the inertia come from one density, so they cannot disagree. The full "
"order, and every property a part can state, is under :ref:`properties`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:371
msgid ""
"The volume a part displaces and that volume's centroid -- its centre of "
"buoyancy -- are always measured from the solid, never stated: a float "
"states 3 g and still pushes aside the whole 8 cm³ of its cube, and is "
"lifted from the cube's middle whatever its stated centre of mass. That "
"difference between where a body balances and where it is lifted is what "
"turns ``buoy`` upright."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:377
msgid ""
"Two cases are worth knowing about. A part that names no material with a "
"density, and states no mass, has no known mass: ``pc info`` says so "
"rather than inventing one, while an export -- and so a simulation -- "
"weighs it at 2700 kg/m³, because an engine handed a body with no mass "
"makes one up regardless. Give it a material. And a part with no solid in "
"it, an open mesh, has no volume to weigh or to buoy."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:385
msgid "What pc info shows"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:387
msgid ""
"``pc info`` reports all of this as ``MassProperties``: each value with "
"its unit and where it came from -- ``stated``, ``the material "
":aluminium``, ``derived: 8000 mm^3 at 2700 kg/m^3``, ``measured``. An "
"assembly reports its parts added up in its own frame and names any part "
"it could not weigh, and the material itself is reported as ``Material``, "
"with its density and its friction. See ``pc info`` in :doc:`cli`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:394
msgid ""
"The values are cached, keyed on the part's geometry and on what they were"
" derived from, so an edit to the CAD, to the material's density or to a "
"stated value is picked up, and an edit to anything else costs nothing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:398
msgid ""
"Every one of these quantities is SI except lengths, which are "
"millimetres, and angles, which are degrees -- the units every engine "
"states them in, so nothing is converted on its way into a model (see the "
"note under :ref:`materials`). They are plain numbers: a unit written into"
" a value, ``\"9.81 m/s^2\"``, is not accepted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:408
msgid "Running a simulation"
msgstr "运行仿真"

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:411
msgid "``pc sim``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:422
msgid ""
"It exits non-zero when a validation does not hold or a run cannot be "
"made. A declaration with no ``validation:`` is run and reported as having"
" run, which is how to look at what a plugin reports before writing the "
"condition. Each run gets a directory of its own under PartCAD's state "
"directory, one per object and simulation and emptied by the next run, "
"holding the scene file the engine was handed, its meshes, whatever the "
"plugin wrote and the pictures it took (see :ref:`sim-snapshots`). See "
"``pc sim`` in :doc:`cli` for every option."
msgstr ""
"当校验不成立或无法完成运行时，它以非零状态退出。没有 ``validation:`` 的声明也会运行，并报告为已运行——"
"这正是在编写条件之前查看插件报告内容的方法。每次运行在 PartCAD 的状态目录下都有自己的目录，每个对象和仿真各一个"
"，下一次运行会将其清空；其中保存着交给引擎的场景文件、它的网格、插件写下的一切，以及插件拍下的图片（参见 :ref:`s"
"im-snapshots` ）。全部选项见 :doc:`cli` 中的 ``pc sim`` 。"

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:430
msgid "Two of those options are about what is asked and what is kept:"
msgstr ""
"其中有两个选项关系到问的是什么、留下的是什么："

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:439
msgid ""
"``--plugin`` runs a simulation with another plugin than the one it names;"
" everything else the declaration says -- the scene, the offset, the "
"parameters, the validation -- is asked of that plugin instead, and the "
"answer is cached as the different question it is. ``--before`` and "
"``--after`` write the two states a validation is evaluated over, as YAML,"
" or as JSON with ``--json`` or a path ending in ``.json``. They write one"
" simulation's, so name the object and, when it declares several, the "
"simulation with ``-f``."
msgstr ""
"``--plugin`` 用另一个插件而不是声明中指定的插件来运行仿真；声明所说的其余一切——场景、偏移、参数、校验——"
"都改为交给这个插件，其答案也作为一个不同的问题来缓存。 ``--before`` 和 ``--after`` 把校验所依"
"据的两个状态写入文件，格式为 YAML；使用 ``--json`` 或路径以 ``.json`` 结尾时则为 JSON。"
"它们只写一项仿真的状态，所以请指明对象，并在它声明了多项仿真时用 ``-f`` 指明是哪一项。"

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:450
msgid "``pc test``'s ``sim`` check"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:452
msgid ""
"``pc test`` holds an object to the same claims every time it runs, as its"
" ``sim`` check -- ``pc test -f sim`` runs that check alone -- so a "
"``simulate:`` is checked whenever the package is tested rather than "
"whenever somebody remembers to ask. It runs a simulation through the same"
" code ``pc sim`` does, and shares its cache."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:458
msgid ""
"It applies to a part or an assembly that declares ``simulate:`` and to "
"nothing else, so a package of bolts pays nothing for it. The verdicts:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:465
msgid "What happened"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:466
msgid "Verdict"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:467
msgid "Every simulation the object declares ran, and every ``validation:`` held"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:468
msgid "pass"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:469
msgid "A ``validation:`` did not hold, or could not be evaluated"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:470 ../../source/simulation.rst:474
msgid "fail"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:471
msgid ""
"A run could not be made: no ``simulation:`` named, a plugin or a scene "
"that cannot be found, a simulator that will not install on this platform,"
" a sandbox that will not build, a crash"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:475
msgid ""
"The plugin names a ``container:`` or a ``dockerImage``, and this machine "
"has no container runtime"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:477
msgid "skip, with a ``WARNING`` carrying the whole report"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:478
msgid "The declaration states no ``validation:``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:479
msgid "skip when a package or a tree is tested; fail when the object is named"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:481
msgid ""
"A failure names the object, the simulation and the reason. Not running is"
" a failure rather than a skip, for the reason the engineering analyses "
"give (see :ref:`engineering-analysis`): the object asked a question, and "
"a plugin that answered nothing has failed. The one excuse is a plugin "
"that was never given the environment it said it needs -- it named an "
"image, and there is nowhere for an image to run. MuJoCo is a wheel and "
"its plugin names no image, so a MuJoCo run that fails has failed on any "
"machine; the Gazebo plugin names one, so a Gazebo run that cannot be made"
" on a machine with no container runtime is skipped. A plugin or a scene "
"that cannot be found is never excused: it is wrong wherever the package "
"is opened."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:492
msgid ""
"A declaration with no ``validation:`` has nothing to be judged by, so "
"``pc test`` does not run it, and what that costs depends on what was "
"asked. Testing a package, it is skipped with a ``WARNING`` saying why: "
"the claim may be one somebody is still writing, and the rest of the "
"package deserves its verdict. Testing the object by name -- ``pc test -a "
"stable`` -- it fails: somebody asked whether this object does what it "
"says, and it says nothing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:499
msgid ""
"``--fast-only`` passes over an object that declares a ``timeout:``, as it"
" does for every check. An object's simulations are checked where the "
"object is tested in its own right, and not again for every assembly whose"
" manufacturability check walks the parts it is made of."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:507
msgid "Pictures of a run"
msgstr ""
"运行的图片"

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:509
msgid ""
"Every run is also asked for two pictures of the scene: one before "
"anything moved and one when the run is over. They are what the IDE's "
"**Validation → Simulation** tab shows side by side, and the one thing a "
"reader can check a verdict against without reading numbers -- a "
"validation that passed for the wrong reason, a block that fell *through* "
"another rather than off it, is plain to see in them."
msgstr ""
"每次运行还会被要求给出两张场景图片：一张在任何东西开始运动之前，一张在运行结束之时。IDE 的 **Validation → "
"Simulation** "
"选项卡并排显示的就是这两张图片；它们也是读者无需阅读数字就能用来核对判定结果的唯一依据——一项因错误原因而通过的校验，一个 *穿过* "
"另一个方块而不是从它上面掉下去的方块，在图片里都一目了然。"

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:516
msgid ""
"They are taken from where the scene is looked at when it is rendered: the"
" ``viewport_origin`` and ``viewport_up`` of its ``render: png:`` "
"configuration, read exactly as ``pc render -t png`` reads them -- the "
"scene's own, or failing that its package's -- and the corner a part is "
"drawn from when neither says anything. So a package that has said how its"
" world is best looked at has said it once, for both:"
msgstr ""
"它们的拍摄位置就是渲染该场景时观察它的位置：即其 ``render: png:`` 配置中的 ``viewport_origin`` 和 "
"``viewport_up`` ，读取方式与 ``pc render -t png`` "
"完全相同——先取场景自己的，没有的话再取其所在包的——两者都没有给出时，则取绘制零件时所用的那个斜角视角。因此，一个包只要说明过它的世界最适合从哪里观看，就只需说明一次，两处通用："

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:537
msgid ""
"The plugin is handed this in the request as ``snapshot`` -- the "
"viewpoint, the picture's ``width`` and ``height`` (the PNG's), and the "
"name of the file each picture goes to, relative to the directory the run "
"may write into -- and says which it drew in its result:"
msgstr ""
"插件在请求中以 ``snapshot`` 的形式收到这些信息——视角、图片的 ``width`` 和 ``height`` （即 PNG "
"的宽高），以及每张图片要写入的文件名（相对于本次运行可以写入的目录）——并在其结果中说明它画出了哪几张："

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:551
msgid ""
"Pictures are optional. A plugin that cannot draw on the machine it runs "
"on, or that predates them, reports none and the run is exactly as good: "
"no verdict depends on a picture. ``pc sim`` says where the pictures of "
"each run were written, and ``--json`` lists them under ``snapshots``. "
"Both plugins draw them from their engine's own geometry at the engine's "
"own poses, on the CPU, so a picture needs no GPU in the sandbox."
msgstr ""
"图片是可选的。在其运行的机器上无法绘图的插件，或者早于这项功能出现的插件，不会报告任何图片，而这次运行同样有效：没有哪个"
"判定结果依赖于图片。 ``pc sim`` 会说明每次运行的图片写到了哪里， ``--json`` 则把它们列在 ``s"
"napshots`` 之下。两个插件都根据各自引擎自己的几何体、按引擎自己的位姿、在 CPU 上绘制这些图片，因此生成图"
"片并不需要沙箱里有 GPU。"

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:561
msgid "Caching runs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:563
msgid ""
"A run is cached the way an engineering analysis is (see \"Analyses, "
"routes and simulations\" under :ref:`caching`): what the plugin reported "
"together with the whole run directory, so asking the same question again "
"puts those files back and runs nothing, and only a run that succeeded is "
"kept."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:568
msgid ""
"For a simulation the question is the scene -- which covers the object, a "
"parameter of it, and so its geometry and everything it is built from -- "
"plus the plugin and every option it resolved to, its environment, the "
"declaration's ``params``, the exporter and its options, the pictures the "
"run is asked to take, and the content of every script involved. A "
"``medium:`` is in it as the facts it resolved to rather than as a name, "
"so a correction to the density of ``:water`` in the package that "
"catalogues it runs the simulation again. The pictures come back with a "
"cached run like any other file it wrote, and a scene looked at from "
"somewhere else is a different question, which runs again."
msgstr ""
"对仿真而言，问题就是场景——它涵盖了对象（场景的一个参数），因而也涵盖了对象的几何体及其构成的一切——再加上插件及其解析"
"出的每一个选项、它的环境、声明中的 ``params`` 、导出器及其选项、要求这次运行拍摄的图片，以及所涉及的每个脚本"
"的内容。 ``medium:`` 以其解析出的事实而非名称计入其中，因此在为 ``:water`` 编目的包中修正其密度"
"，会让仿真重新运行。图片与缓存的运行一同取回，就像它写下的任何其他文件一样；而从别处观察的场景是另一个问题，会重新运行。"

#: ../../source/simulation.rst:578
msgid ""
"A ``validation:`` is not part of the question: editing one re-judges the "
"cached run rather than repeating it, in ``pc sim`` and in ``pc test`` "
"alike. ``pc test``'s verdict is cached too, keyed on the declarations as "
"written, ``validation:`` included, and on the key of every run it was "
"judged on, so nothing else changes it. A skip, a run that did not "
"deliver, a declaration that does not resolve and one with no "
"``validation:`` are worked out afresh every time."
msgstr ""

